2186173356 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173356 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173356 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173356 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "861060f5-833b-53df-b3a5-d6ea7eec998b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173357 #edb/ref 1080 "r = \\frac{3π}{4}θ" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173357 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173357 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aca9b9cd-5c0a-5514-b70a-3484910d2831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173358 #edb/ref 1080 "z = \\frac{3π}{4}r" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4d708de-3f2c-591a-b70f-f80405f0592b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173359 #edb/ref 1080 "θ = \\frac{3π}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173359 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173359 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1a49595-c2e6-5a29-b0bc-b04ce030cc21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173360 #edb/ref 1080 "z = \\frac{3π}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173360 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173360 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c89aabd-f3e7-5e77-8cdd-283470a56de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173361 #edb/ref 1080 "r = \\frac{3π}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173361 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173361 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7fe143ca-1333-56b7-ab47-3dea4ff46d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175814 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175814 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52199b9c-bb04-5c14-b4ca-909d46c7f039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175814 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $3$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta=3$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2908fb8953f692c596822ecf3fb6454cc5f02f1620f3936d2f5a198d0e9a380d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175815 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175815 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1ea30ad7159ada825af44e58f3747f378815e572c0b18d3e8916fd2a6eec58cf.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee821db7-b420-5f37-a54c-a0bbe6cc83be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175815 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the distance from any point on our surface to the $z$-axis equals $5,$ we must have $r=5$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $r = 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175826 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175826 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=4r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f948d39d-6d7d-56b8-bb2c-b0a74ac514b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175826 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=4r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} = \\dfrac{r}{4r} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175964 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175965 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175976 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1045 "Surfaces in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176119 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "503ab0f0-15ff-5bd2-ac55-6138d76918dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176181 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cbebe6f51a470f8ef43297c3e36401bd9c6f97fe05b1a1a96c5438fc87475e59.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "217ef9fa-d664-5e65-832a-13cbccc57fc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e14b0f3-9fb0-5b36-9ab8-66e50f0fd598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176182 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{r}=1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176183 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/57/57ab42c2a44ff953bd752455c69f15039d7f0cd4be92c9050063adebc8b3aad3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "954c8b7b-4fdc-58e2-a883-e094440515d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176669 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176669 #edb/ref 1061 23340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176669 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e3fef5c-90c1-5ae0-8dd6-f78ba4880000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176669 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176698 #edb/ref 1061 32906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa14dab6-6b2c-5517-80c3-face079514f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176699 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176699 #edb/ref 1061 32907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176699 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce566092-6969-5011-a590-ad519430622e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176699 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176712 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176712 #edb/ref 1061 33203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "605436cb-a92c-5db6-8a0c-283fbd5bc112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176713 #edb/ref 1061 33206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3218f617-0ff9-5905-a133-72f0abbba8d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176714 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176714 #edb/ref 1061 33207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c69de56-41f5-581e-b36e-f6ebf219b307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176715 #edb/ref 1061 33211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7f59e46-b37b-5d77-aa4e-867545d8bca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176973 #edb/ref 1064 "In a rectangular (or Cartesian) system of coordinates, we can think of a point as the vertex of a rectangular solid. For instance, the point $P(1,\\sqrt3,3)$ can be represented as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47873274f1cc630a8275b3ef2f83210b3d16a4f3d0acabeae9dbda32844a9adf.png)\n\nWhen we consider the corresponding cylindrical coordinates of $P,$ given by $\\left(2, \\dfrac{\\pi}3, 3\\right)$ in this case, we can think of $P$ as a point that lies on the edge of a cylinder, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fefa7c5fac5cac803fdebeaa9eab4233d723b452d2ee8dc17151d992064223f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176973 #edb/ref 1065 3941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176973 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7224d97c-31fc-5a6c-986c-9f8fd6dae5de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176973 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176985 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWhen we fix one of these coordinates and allow the others to vary, we obtain some simple surfaces.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nr=c, \\qquad\n$$\n\n where $c\\geq 0$ requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis is fixed while $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. So, we obtain a circular cylinder.\n\n![](images/72/728776f0f04642a1596df605968cb5dc5df22ea0a401cf9522ba1ff91839ff0e.png)\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n\\theta=c\n$$\n\n where $c\\in [0,2\\pi)$ requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. This constructs a **half-plane** that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/bd/bdc54b001fde4816ced9eff3a771bdd5900804f78b3bd5e9586db42bebe80297.png)\n\n- Finally, the equation \n\n$$\nz=c\n$$\n\n where $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ requires that the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface is fixed while $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. Therefore, this surface is a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/c3/c3f0af5747690797f27e156aa281c0b24f600919961a63a0244febaca710fcbe.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176985 #edb/ref 1065 5308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16974c3a-8773-51dd-9c56-6407a423727f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176985 #edb/ref 1067 "Describing Simple Surfaces in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176986 #edb/ref 1064 "What surface is defined by the equation $z^2=r^2$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?\n\nAgain, recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz^2=r^2\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis.\nIn other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis, which is given by $\\dfrac{r}{z}$, must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} =\\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{z^2}} = \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{r^2}} = \\pm1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\n- For $\\dfrac{r}{z} = 1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for positive $z{:}$\n\n![](images/18/1841d50bb07c0e6787f4824af0e13516912fb9ad77fac0515f00c243899eb18e.png)\n\n- On the other hand, for $\\dfrac{r}{z} = -1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for negative $z{:}$\n\n![](images/e3/e32b1b4ba84faea7a4dc536e0f49bcda9295a4a42790162b3b9bad06db39a908.png)\n\nTherefore, combining the two half cones, the equation $z^2 = r^2$ defines the *cone* shown below.\n\n![](images/84/842f8cf967d10144e568804bdfb1aadf75f029cf87dd7b6785168a44a95d0615.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176986 #edb/ref 1065 5309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "795b19b5-f3e0-535a-99a9-cd09e1c2cb58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176986 #edb/ref 1067 "Cones in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176987 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have kept the domains of the plane polar coordinates restricted to $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$ What happens if we lift these restrictions?\n\nConsider the equation $r=\\theta$ with the domain restrictions $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ which defines the surface shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84737b12a1689fe2ac03ab09572e4c396d35493f55df5d65af80eeb8c5ae5676.png)\n\nSuppose we remove the restriction $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ so that it can be any number. What would the surface look like then?\n\nIf we remove the restriction on $\\theta,$ we obtain more spirals. For example, the diagram below shows $r=\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 4\\pi).$\n\n![](images/56/568291daf47340265d97a121f0b7b927233f2698e87e0d5adebbba640ff20afe.png)\n\nWe can also lift the restriction $r \\geq 0$ so $r$ can take negative values. Typically, allowing negative values of $r$ adds $\\pi$ to the parameter $\\theta.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(|r|,\\theta ),\\, & r\\ge 0, \\\\ (|r|,\\theta +\\pi ),\\, & r<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176987 #edb/ref 1065 5310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8c45bbba-d4b2-5559-8236-d79eb000c27b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176987 #edb/ref 1067 "Relaxing Variable Constraints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236419 #edb/ref 1080 "A sphere" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236419 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236419 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f594307-11ca-55e6-89fb-478c28a7f536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236553 #edb/ref 1080 "A half-cone" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "754cf801-74ad-5792-8ad1-2fb52740f26c" #edb/ref 1759218604541