92186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186240062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240763 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f652d0b6-869b-50eb-b174-ab5aac5a2029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240767 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb1b76b8-a67e-5415-bdac-15fb92d4d333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/24/2499715807dedd0234363454dea33afb592d497005c4f643b03145b6c89cc222.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bdd46c9-96f1-5f6a-ab6b-a539338b4af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240824 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c4d6b6fe6b594192040f6dc6c3a37b7eacff4387dc31d2d9e5f353fefd5c4663.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b59c20b1-4301-50d7-bd7e-48ab54116dca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240827 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $0,$ we must have $z=0$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e8ed066-cedf-5856-9a34-59e242391831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240828 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ede99d4b-4522-5a3b-b04a-ad1a84dcbbb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240830 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a63f55a-8c70-5d95-83cc-5a9cc3779061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240833 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ee/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "773ba210-d4fa-5958-a8ac-1084763e8c5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240835 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11d1bb30-6c4f-5361-a860-88f0d5525276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240849 #edb/ref 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
